## 10. Orchestra Wrapper Conflict Detection

Orchestra wrapper pulls Spring Boot transitively. After updating both the
explicit Spring Boot version and the Orchestra wrapper version:

1. Confirm the Orchestra wrapper’s internal Spring Boot version is equal to or
   lower than the approved Boot version being set in the project
1. If Orchestra `4.1.5` pulls Boot `3.5.12` internally and the project sets
   Boot `3.4.3`, the higher Orchestra-internal version wins at runtime — flag
   this as a VERSION CONFLICT in the report and ask the human which version
   takes precedence
1. Do not remove or override Orchestra’s transitive Spring Boot pull without
   explicit human approval

-----

## 11. Black Duck Scan Interpretation

1. Extract each flagged component: artifact ID, current version, CVE IDs
1. Look up each artifact ID in the PAA sheet
1. If the sheet-approved version is higher than the current version, apply the
   version change per Section 9
1. If the component is BOM-managed and the Boot BOM upgrade resolves it to the
   sheet-approved version, the BOM upgrade alone is sufficient — do not add a
   redundant explicit override
1. If the component is NOT in the PAA sheet, do not change its version — flag
   as NOT IN SHEET / REQUIRES SEPARATE REVIEW
1. Record each CVE, artifact ID, old version, new version, and resolution method
   (BOM upgrade or explicit override) in the remediation report

-----

## 12. Multi-Module Project Rules

- Map the full version inheritance chain before making any change
- Update only the authoritative version declaration per module hierarchy
- If a child module pins a Spring component independently:
  - Flag it explicitly before touching it
  - Do not silently remove the pin without human confirmation
- After all changes, verify no child module retains a Spring component pinned
  to a version older than the sheet-approved version for that artifact
- In a Gradle multi-project build, check `buildSrc` and any included builds for
  additional Spring version declarations

-----

## 13. Validation Steps

Perform after all changes — without running the build unless the human asks:

1. Every changed version string matches the approved PAA sheet version exactly,
   including format (numeric, `.RELEASE` suffix, `-M1` suffix)
1. No Spring artifact remains explicitly pinned to a version older than the
   sheet-approved version for its major line
1. Orchestra wrapper version updated if present
1. Spring Cloud BOM version updated if present and sheet-approved version exists
1. No non-Spring dependencies were modified
1. No application source or test files were modified
1. No `application.yml` / `application.properties` files were modified
1. No SNAPSHOT versions were changed
1. If the human runs `mvn dependency:tree` or `./gradlew dependencies` and
   shares the output, scan every `org.springframework.*`,
   `org.springframework.boot.*`, `org.springframework.cloud.*`,
   `org.springframework.security.*`, and `org.springframework.data.*` line —
   flag any artifact resolving to a version older than the sheet-approved
   version for that artifact
1. Confirm no duplicate BOM imports were introduced

-----

## 14. Post-Remediation OSS Validation Guidance

After build changes are complete and the build passes:

1. Trigger a new Black Duck scan on the repository
1. Confirm all previously flagged Spring CVEs no longer appear in the scan
   results
1. If any CVE still appears after the upgrade, stop and report it as UNRESOLVED
   — do not mark the remediation complete
1. Complete the OSS validation process per the internal OSS validation workflow
1. Record the Black Duck scan run ID and result in the remediation report
1. Deadline: all testing, patching, validation, and OSS validation must be
   complete by June 9, 2026 at 11:59 PM ET

